---
/**
 * ServiceCardsBlock — the `service_cards` layout: the service-tile grids that
 * carry the four hub pages.
 *
 * EXTRACTED, NOT DESIGNED. Five live bands, read element for element:
 *
 *   src/pages/cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:282-317  `#services`, paper,
 *       `.svc-grid` three across, six linked tiles, closing `.svc-outro`.
 *   src/pages/general-dentistry/index.astro:251-280   `#services`, paper,
 *       `.svc-grid-4` four across of `<article class="svc-feat">` — the
 *       FEATURES shape: no link, no arrow, no hover-reveal, and a body that
 *       carries an inline `<a class="vs-link">` to /emergency-dentistry/.
 *   src/pages/services/index.astro:163-204   `#cosmetic`, charcoal
 *       (`class="section alt"`), `.svc-grid.svc-grid-6` three across, with the
 *       CTA pair INSIDE the section head.
 *   src/pages/services/index.astro:207-249   `#implants`, sage-pale
 *       (`class="section dark"`), `.svc-grid.svc-grid-5` — five tiles centred
 *       by a six-column grid — CTA pair in the head, second button the phone.
 *   src/pages/services/index.astro:252-286   `#everyday`, paper,
 *       `.svc-grid.svc-grid-2`, the only band whose tiles carry a `.svc-tag`.
 *   src/pages/implant-dentistry/index.astro:346-366  the tile grid inside
 *       `#solutions`, NESTED — see below.
 *
 * Two card SHAPES and two PLACEMENTS, which is why one layout is this long.
 *
 * ── TILES vs FEATURES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A tile is `<a class="svc-card compact" data-vs-arrow>`: a photograph with a
 * dark gradient over it, the title and an <ArrowBadge> along the bottom, and a
 * description that slides open on hover. A feature is `<article class="svc-feat">`:
 * a white card, picture on top, sage title and always-visible body under it,
 * and NO link at all. general-dentistry's four are features precisely because
 * one of their bodies contains a link of its own — a link inside a link is
 * invalid, and a description that only appears on hover would hide it.
 *
 * They are one layout and not two because they are one editorial idea ("the
 * services in this category") and because `cardStyle` is the only field that
 * differs: every other field means the same thing in both shapes. `href` and
 * `tag` have no rendering in the features shape and are ignored there, which is
 * recorded rather than hidden — see `cardStyle` in Props.
 *
 * ── `href` blank means the tile is not a link ────────────────────────────
 * An `<a>` with no href is not a link, and an arrow badge on something that
 * goes nowhere advertises a destination that does not exist. So a tile with a
 * blank `href` is drawn as `<article class="svc-card compact">` with neither
 * `data-vs-arrow` nor the badge: the card, its picture and its words survive;
 * only the affordance goes. Every tile on all five live bands has an href, so
 * no band in the corpus takes this path today.
 *
 * ── `body` is html-bearing, `subFoot` too ───────────────────────────────
 * general-dentistry's Emergency card and cosmetic-dentistry's `.svc-outro`
 * both carry an inline `<a class="vs-link">` mid-sentence, so both render
 * `set:html`. The tiles' plain-prose descriptions render byte-identically
 * either way — `set:html` is the shape the CMS field has, not a widening.
 *
 * NO `.vs-link` RULE LIVES HERE, for the reason ComparisonCardsBlock:922 and
 * TechGridBlock record: a link arriving through `set:html` is already styled by
 * global.css:39-49, and the hub sheets tint it for their own ground. A local
 * rule would be a compound this block authors — it would outrank both and take
 * the site's own link treatment away from a band that had it right.
 *
 * ── The CTA pair sits in the HEAD, not at the foot ──────────────────────
 * services/index.astro:168-177 puts `.cta-row.in-head` inside `.section-head`,
 * under the intro paragraph, and that is the only placement this family ships.
 * It is not the foot row card_grid and pricing_tiers draw; a band with both
 * would be a shape no page has. Hence one row, in the head, and none when the
 * block is nested (a guest must not grow a row its host band never had — the
 * rule PricingTiersBlock:255 states for the same reason).
 *
 * THE PHONE RULE. `#implants`' second button is `Call {phoneLabel}` built from
 * src/data/contact.ts (services/index.astro:216-218). When a cta href stores
 * the token "phone" the visible label is rebuilt here from that same constant
 * and the STORED label is only the on/off switch. A phone number typed into a
 * WordPress field is a phone number that goes stale in a WordPress field, and
 * this site already has one place to change it — Practice Settings.
 *
 * ── NESTED: the grid as a guest ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * implant-dentistry.astro:346 draws its tile grid INSIDE `#solutions`, after
 * the price table, as a sibling in the same `.wrap`. With `nested` truthy this
 * block therefore emits no `<section>`, no `.wrap`, no `id` and no
 * `data-vs-reveal-batch` — the band, the anchor and the reveal batch are the
 * host's — and reaches the page through the host's `<slot />`. Same contract as
 * PricingTiersBlock's nested mode, read from that file; the difference is that
 * teeth-whitening's template ships a sub-head there and implant-dentistry's
 * grid has none, so the head here is drawn only if an editor actually fills it
 * (as an `<h3>`: the host's `<h2>` is already the band's heading).
 *
 * `nested` is read for TRUTH, never `=== true`: an ACF true_false nobody has
 * saved comes back null and an importer may write `1`.
 *
 * The 56px gap between the price table and the grid is the whole reason the
 * flag exists. A `<section>` of its own would put 220px there (110px top and
 * bottom) and the page would visibly move — WordPress is here to change the
 * words, not the design.
 *
 * ── OPTIONAL EVERYTHING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every field is optional and a blank one draws NOTHING, never an empty
 * wrapper: no cards, no grid element; no picture, no `.svc-img`; no title and
 * no link, no `.svc-row-bot`. Each of those wrappers is a grid or flex child
 * with a gap, so an empty one is a visible strip, not a collapsed nothing.
 *
 * NOTHING HERE THROWS. An editor triggers deploys and never sees the output
 * (cms/mu-plugins/vs-deploy.php:142-158), so every field is read as if it might
 * be missing, blank or the wrong type. Same rule as PageBlocks.astro.
 *
 * Styles are co-located per docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.5 — every rule below names
 * the sheet and line it was copied out of, and the <style> header explains the
 * three places the four hub sheets disagree and which value won.
 *
 * Spec: docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md Phase 4, Wave A.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import ArrowBadge from "../components/ArrowBadge.astro";
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import { phoneLabel } from "../data/contact";
import { resolveCta } from "./cta";

/** The card's image sub-field, as wpgraphql-acf returns an ACF image. Same
    shape as MediaSplitBlock.astro:125-132 and TechGridBlock's, and for the same
    reason: the manifest selects `image { node { sourceUrl altText mediaDetails
    { width height } } }`. */
export interface ServiceCardsImage {
  node?: {
    sourceUrl?: string | null;
    altText?: string | null;
    mediaDetails?: { width?: number | null; height?: number | null } | null;
  } | null;
}

/** One row of the layout's `svc_cards` repeater. Every sub-field is optional at
    the schema level — ACF returns null for anything an editor never saved. */
export interface ServiceCard {
  title?: string | null;
  /** May carry an inline `<a class="vs-link">`. Drawn `set:html`. */
  body?: string | null;
  /** The pill over the picture — `.svc-tag`. Only /services/#everyday has one. */
  tag?: string | null;
  /** Where the tile goes. Blank draws the card unlinked — see the header. */
  href?: string | null;
  /**
   * An `object-position` override for the picture, e.g. "center 30%".
   * services/index.astro:232,275 pass exactly this through to the `<img>` on
   * the cards whose subject sits off-centre; every other card omits it and gets
   * the `object-fit: cover` default.
   */
  imagePos?: string | null;
  /** Alt override. Falls back to the alt stored on the file in WordPress. */
  imageAlt?: string | null;
  image?: ServiceCardsImage | null;
}

interface Props {
  /** DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. */
  id?: string;
  /** The same value as `id`. Accepted so the spread in PageBlocks is lossless. */
  anchor?: string | null;
  /** Rail label. Read by the page that builds the rail, not by this band. */
  navLabel?: string | null;
  band?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3). */
  heading?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;
  /**
   * Guest mode. Truthy, never `=== true` — see the header. Ignored fields in
   * this mode are `band`, `anchor`/`id` and the CTA seven, exactly as
   * PricingTiersBlock ignores them: they belong to the host band.
   */
  nested?: boolean | null;
  /**
   * "tiles" | "features". NULL means tiles: the tile is the shape five of the
   * six live bands ship, and a row saved by any path that skips the select must
   * degrade to the common case rather than to the one page's exception.
   */
  cardStyle?: string | null;
  /** "2" | "3" | "4" | "5" — ACF returns a select's value as a string. */
  columns?: string | number | null;
  /**
   * The width at which this grid collapses to a single column: "640" or "780".
   *
   * A FIELD RATHER THAN A CONSTANT BECAUSE THE PAGES GENUINELY DISAGREE, and
   * no other field can tell them apart. cosmetic-dentistry.css:975,
   * implant-dentistry.css:1502 and general-dentistry.css:1339 collapse at
   * 640; services.css:520 collapses at 780 — and /services/ #cosmetic and
   * /cosmetic-dentistry/ #services are BOTH six tiles at columns=3, identical
   * in every stored value. Picking one silently would change what a visitor
   * sees between 640px and 780px on whichever page lost, which is a design
   * change, and this migration changes the words and not the design.
   * Blank = "640", the majority.
   */
  collapseAt?: string | null;
  svcCards?: (ServiceCard | null)[] | null;
  /**
   * The closing line under the grid — `.svc-outro`. Its own field and not a
   * second `body` because they are two places in the markup: `body` is the
   * band's intro above the cards, this is the sentence below them. May carry an
   * inline link (cosmetic-dentistry.astro:314 does).
   */
  subFoot?: string | null;
  /** The block_cta_fields() seven. See the header for the href policy. */
  ctaLabel?: string | null;
  ctaHref?: string | null;
  /** Hover override. Blank falls back to ./cta.ts's destination table. */
  ctaHover?: string | null;
  ctaLabel2?: string | null;
  ctaHref2?: string | null;
  ctaHover2?: string | null;
  /** The small line beside the buttons. */
  ctaNote?: string | null;
}

/**
 * band value -> the modifier class the pages' markup already uses.
 *
 * `paper` maps to nothing because a paper band is a bare `class="section"` on
 * every page in the family. `alt` and `dark` are inherited names and inherited
 * liabilities — `.section.alt` is charcoal on services.css:126 and cream on
 * teeth-whitening.css (Fact 4) — which is why every colour in <style> below is
 * written against the band VALUE and the class is only the hook the
 * un-migrated hub sheets still need.
 */
const BAND_CLASS: Record<string, string | undefined> = {
  paper: undefined,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

/**
 * `<Image>` inputs by shape and column count.
 *
 * Not one set for the whole layout: a tile in a two-across grid is twice the
 * rendered width of one in a five-across grid, and handing both the same
 * `widths` ships either a blurry hero or a 1200px file inside a 273px card.
 * The three live values are the templates' own — cosmetic-dentistry.astro:298
 * and implant-dentistry.astro:353 for three across, services.astro:270 for two,
 * general-dentistry.astro:264 for the features row — and the counts no page
 * ships take the same widths with the `sizes` this component's own breakpoints
 * imply (1100px -> two across, 640px -> one).
 *
 * The `sizes` strings for three across differ between the hubs and the services
 * page (`(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 33vw` there); the one below is the one that
 * matches the breakpoints in <style>, which is the only version of the string
 * that can be right about this component.
 */
const IMAGE_SPEC: Record<string, { widths: number[]; sizes: string }> = {
  "tiles-2": { widths: [600, 900, 1200], sizes: "(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw" },
  "tiles-3": {
    widths: [420, 640, 900],
    sizes: "(max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 1100px) 50vw, 33vw",
  },
  "tiles-4": {
    widths: [420, 640, 900],
    sizes: "(max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 1100px) 50vw, 25vw",
  },
  "tiles-5": {
    widths: [420, 640, 900],
    sizes: "(max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 1100px) 50vw, 33vw",
  },
  "features-2": { widths: [300, 480, 640], sizes: "(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw" },
  "features-3": {
    widths: [300, 480, 640],
    sizes: "(max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 1100px) 50vw, 33vw",
  },
  "features-4": {
    widths: [300, 480, 640],
    sizes: "(max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 1100px) 50vw, 280px",
  },
  "features-5": {
    widths: [300, 480, 640],
    sizes: "(max-width: 640px) 100vw, (max-width: 1100px) 50vw, 25vw",
  },
};

const {
  id,
  band = "paper",
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  nested,
  cardStyle,
  columns,
  collapseAt,
  svcCards,
  subFoot,
  ctaLabel,
  ctaHref,
  ctaHover,
  ctaLabel2,
  ctaHref2,
  ctaHover2,
  ctaNote,
} = Astro.props;

/** Trim once, here, so every guard below tests for real content rather than for
 *  a field an editor left as a single space. */
const text = (input: unknown): string => (typeof input === "string" ? input.trim() : "");

const isNested = Boolean(nested);

const bandKey = typeof band === "string" && band !== "" ? band : "paper";
const bandClass = BAND_CLASS[bandKey];

/**
 * The eyebrow carries `light` on the two sage bands and only there —
 * services/index.astro:210 spells `class="eyebrow light"` on the sage-pale
 * `#implants` head and plain `class="eyebrow"` on the charcoal `#cosmetic` one
 * (:165). Inert under the current cascade, since `.section.dark .eyebrow`
 * (0,3,0) already beats `.eyebrow.light` (0,2,0), but it is in the HTML, so it
 * has to be here or the diff that verifies this phase is not empty.
 */
const eyebrowLight = bandKey === "sage-pale" || bandKey === "sage";

const isFeatures = text(cardStyle) === "features";

/**
 * The column count, and the class that carries it.
 *
 * `cols-N` is this component's own modifier, not a page's, and it is emitted
 * ALWAYS — including for the count each family treats as its default. That is
 * deliberate and it is the one place this file departs from the hub markup:
 *
 *   - the four hubs spell the same three-across grid four different ways
 *     (`svc-grid` on two pages, `svc-grid svc-grid-6` on a third), and a
 *     `columns` field cannot honestly write `-6`, which counts CARDS;
 *   - more importantly, `grid-template-columns` is exactly where the hub sheets
 *     disagree — `.impd .svc-grid` is six columns where `.cdent .svc-grid` is
 *     three — and a single-class rule of this component's compiles to
 *     `.svc-grid[data-astro-cid-…]`, (0,2,0), which TIES with `.impd .svc-grid`
 *     and leaves bundle order to decide the geometry of a live client page.
 *     `.svc-grid.cols-3` is (0,3,0) and cannot tie with any of them.
 *
 * The family base classes are kept as the pages spell them (`svc-grid`,
 * `svc-grid-4`) so the un-migrated hub sheets keep styling the band during the
 * transition, exactly as CardGridBlock keeps `.why-grid`.
 *
 * Default 3 for tiles and 4 for features: the count each shape actually ships,
 * so a row that somehow saved without a `columns` value degrades to the layout
 * the markup already has rather than to the editor-facing default.
 */
const COLUMN_CHOICES = ["2", "3", "4", "5"];
const rawColumns = String(columns ?? "").trim();
const cols = COLUMN_CHOICES.includes(rawColumns) ? rawColumns : isFeatures ? "4" : "3";
const colsClass = `cols-${cols}`;

/* The templates key their grids off the CARD COUNT, not the column count —
   services/index.astro writes `svc-grid svc-grid-6` for a SIX-tile grid drawn
   three across. Dropping it changed the class attribute on three live bands and
   orphaned services.css:235-255 and its breakpoints, so it is emitted here too.
   It is a hook, not a layout: this component still owns grid-template-columns. */
const countClass = `svc-grid-${(Array.isArray(svcCards) ? svcCards : []).filter(Boolean).length}`;

/* Blank = 640, which is what three of the four sheets do. */
const collapseWidth = String(collapseAt ?? "").trim() === "780" ? "780" : "640";

let imageSpec = IMAGE_SPEC[`${isFeatures ? "features" : "tiles"}-${cols}`];

/* THE SIZES HINT TRACKS THE COLLAPSE, because on every page the two agree:
   a band that goes one-column at 780 (services/index.astro:190,:234) also
   ships `(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 33vw`, while a band that goes one-column at
   640 ships the 640/1100 three-stop form. Deriving it from `collapseAt` rather
   than exposing a second field keeps one decision in wp-admin instead of two
   that can contradict each other. Wrong `sizes` is not a layout bug — it makes
   the browser pick the wrong candidate, so the image is soft or wasteful — but
   it is still a change to a live page, which is why it is not left as a
   constant. */
if (collapseWidth === "780" && !isFeatures) {
  imageSpec = {
    widths: imageSpec.widths,
    sizes: cols === "2" ? "(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 50vw" : "(max-width: 991px) 100vw, 33vw",
  };
}

/**
 * CMS rows -> cards.
 *
 * The filter drops a row with no title, no body, no tag and no picture. That is
 * an abandoned "Add a card" and it is the one input that cannot be drawn as
 * anything: an empty card is a 380px-tall dark rectangle in the middle of a
 * grid. A row with ANY of the four is kept — the rest is content the editor
 * still owes, and hiding the card would lose the part they did write.
 *
 * `Array.isArray` first because an unqueried repeater arrives `undefined` and
 * one an editor never opened arrives `null`, and `.map` on either throws during
 * the build of whatever page the band sits on.
 */
const rows = (Array.isArray(svcCards) ? svcCards : [])
  .map((row) => {
    const node = row?.image?.node ?? null;
    const src = typeof node?.sourceUrl === "string" ? node.sourceUrl : "";
    const width = node?.mediaDetails?.width ?? 0;
    const height = node?.mediaDetails?.height ?? 0;

    return {
      title: text(row?.title),
      body: text(row?.body),
      tag: text(row?.tag),
      href: text(row?.href),
      imagePos: text(row?.imagePos),
      /* Editor override first, then the alt text stored on the file itself. */
      alt: text(row?.imageAlt) || text(node?.altText),
      src,
      width,
      height,
      /* `<Image>` refuses a remote source without explicit intrinsic dimensions,
         so a media item WordPress recorded no size for — an SVG, or an upload
         that did not finish — cannot be rendered at all. That degrades to a
         card with no picture rather than failing a build an editor triggered
         (the rule MediaSplitBlock:485-506 and UnknownBlock both run on). */
      hasImage: src !== "" && width > 0 && height > 0,
    };
  })
  .filter((card) => card.title !== "" || card.body !== "" || card.tag !== "" || card.hasImage);

const missingImages = rows.filter((card) => !card.hasImage).length;

if (missingImages > 0) {
  console.warn(
    `[blocks] service_cards on ${Astro.url.pathname}${id ? `#${id}` : ""} has ${missingImages} ` +
      "card(s) with no usable picture — drawing those cards without one",
  );
}

const eyebrowText = text(eyebrow);
const headingHtml = text(heading);
const bodyText = text(body);
const subFootHtml = text(subFoot);

/* The token map, hover table and resolvers live in ./cta.ts — ONE copy for
   every block that draws a CTA row. The tables only work because the blocks
   agree, and a per-block copy is one more place for the next hover-label edit
   to miss. */
const cta1 = resolveCta(ctaHref, ctaHover);
const cta2 = resolveCta(ctaHref2, ctaHover2);

/**
 * THE PHONE RULE. A stored destination of "phone" rebuilds the visible label
 * from src/data/contact.ts, so the number on the button is the number in
 * Practice Settings and can never be a stale one somebody typed into a page
 * field. The stored label still decides WHETHER the button is drawn — it is the
 * on/off switch, not the words.
 */
const callLabel = `Call ${phoneLabel}`;
const isPhone = (rawHref?: string | null) => text(rawHref) === "phone";
const label1 = isPhone(ctaHref) ? callLabel : text(ctaLabel);
const label2 = isPhone(ctaHref2) ? callLabel : text(ctaLabel2);

/**
 * The row draws for EITHER button, not just the first — a band that fills only
 * the second pair must not silently lose it. Never when nested: the guest draws
 * no head, and a button row with no head to sit in is a shape no page ships.
 */
const hasCta = text(ctaLabel) !== "";
const hasCta2 = text(ctaLabel2) !== "";
const hasCtaRow = (hasCta || hasCta2) && !isNested;
const ctaNoteText = text(ctaNote);

/**
 * Button variants follow the band, and the pairing is read off the two live
 * rows rather than derived from how light the ground looks: BOTH bands that
 * ship this row use light + ghost-light, the charcoal `#cosmetic`
 * (services.astro:170-175) and the PALE sage `#implants` (:213-218). So sage-pale
 * takes the dark pairing here even though its ground is pale — that is what the
 * live page renders. Paper and cream take the family's light-band pairing
 * (sage + ghost), which is what card_grid and media_split draw on those bands.
 */
const isLightGround = bandKey === "paper" || bandKey === "cream";
const primaryVariant = isLightGround ? "sage" : "light";
const secondaryVariant = isLightGround ? "ghost" : "ghost-light";

const hasHead = eyebrowText !== "" || headingHtml !== "" || bodyText !== "" || hasCtaRow;

/**
 * A row with nothing in it draws nothing at all — not even the band.
 *
 * This is the one place this block goes further than its siblings, which all
 * emit their `<section>` unconditionally. It goes further because the shell is
 * not free: `.section` carries 110px of padding top AND bottom, so an empty
 * band is a 220px hole in the middle of a live client page, and the person who
 * added the row and has not filled it yet is the person who triggers the
 * deploy. An empty wrapper is not a collapsed nothing here, so it is not drawn.
 */
const hasContent =
  eyebrowText !== "" ||
  headingHtml !== "" ||
  bodyText !== "" ||
  hasCtaRow ||
  rows.length > 0 ||
  subFootHtml !== "";

/**
 * The guest's own head.
 *
 * No page ships one — implant-dentistry's nested grid follows the price table
 * with nothing between them — so this draws only when an editor has actually
 * filled a head field on a nested row. Dropping those words instead would be
 * the fault this project keeps naming: a field that is queried and never
 * rendered reads as supported. The one live nested row leaves all three blank
 * and draws nothing at all.
 */
const hasNestedHeadCopy = eyebrowText !== "" || headingHtml !== "" || bodyText !== "";
const hasNestedHead = isNested && hasNestedHeadCopy;
---

{
  /* ONE grid expression, TWO placements. The band and the guest differ only in
     what wraps the grid, so the grid is written once inside this IIFE and both
     branches return it — the construction CardGridBlock:355 uses, and for the
     reason it gives there: inlined copies of a card map are how renderers
     drift. The whole template is this one expression, so every text node in the
     output is one written below. */
  (() => {
    /* A BLANK GUEST DRAWS NOTHING; A BLANK BAND DRAWS ITS SHELL.
       The asymmetry is the point. A nested guest has no id and no rail entry,
       so emitting nothing for it costs nothing. A BAND does have an anchor and
       a nav_label, and the eight registered blocks all emit
       `<section><div class="wrap"></div></section>` when empty — a block that
       emits nothing instead leaves a rail chip pointing at an id nothing
       rendered, and because the rail loops count every non-blank anchor, the
       missing id also drifts the `-2` dedupe suffix of every anchor after it.
       One inconsistent block is a whole page's rail, so the band shell is
       drawn for consistency with its siblings, not for tidiness. */
    if (!hasContent && isNested) return null;

    const cards =
      rows.length > 0 &&
      (isFeatures ? (
        /* general-dentistry.astro:261-278. A feature is an article, has no
           link, no tag and no arrow: the four fields a tile uses for its
           affordance have no rendering in this shape. */
        <div class:list={["svc-grid-4", colsClass, isNested && "nested"]}>
          {rows.map((card) => (
            <article class="svc-feat">
              {card.hasImage && (
                <div class="svc-feat-img">
                  <Image
                    src={card.src}
                    width={card.width}
                    height={card.height}
                    alt={card.alt}
                    widths={imageSpec.widths}
                    sizes={imageSpec.sizes}
                    style={card.imagePos ? `object-position: ${card.imagePos}` : undefined}
                  />
                </div>
              )}{
                /* Flush `}{` against the picture's conditional, no newline
                   between them: Astro preserves whitespace between expressions,
                   so a conditional on its own line leaves a whitespace-only
                   text node in the card even when the picture is missing. Same
                   idiom as CardGridBlock:365 and StatCalloutBlock:224. */
                (card.title !== "" || card.body !== "") && (
                  <div class="svc-feat-body">
                    {card.title !== "" && <h3>{card.title}</h3>}{
                      /* Flush again: general-dentistry's four cards all carry
                         both halves, and a card with only a title must not gain
                         a 12px gap row for a paragraph that is not there. */
                      card.body !== "" && <p class="svc-feat-desc" set:html={card.body} />
                    }
                  </div>
                )
              }
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      ) : (
        /* cosmetic-dentistry.astro:292-311, services.astro:183-202 and
           implant-dentistry.astro:347-365 — the same tile to the character,
           plus the `.svc-tag` services.astro:274 adds on `#everyday`. */
        <div class:list={["svc-grid", countClass, colsClass, collapseWidth === "780" && "at-780", isNested && "nested"]}>
          {rows.map((card) => {
            /* An `<a>` with no href is not a link — see the header. The badge
               and `data-vs-arrow` go with it, because both exist to promise a
               destination. */
            const linked = card.href !== "";
            const hasRowBot = card.title !== "" || linked;

            /* The interior is built ONCE and the two elements below are two
               literal tags rather than one `<Tag>` variable. A dynamic tag name
               sends every attribute through Astro's runtime serialiser, which
               writes `data-vs-arrow="true"` and `data-astro-cid-…="true"` where
               a literal tag writes both bare — five live bands' worth of tiles
               differing from the template in a byte no page asked to change. */
            const inner = (
              <Fragment>
                {card.hasImage && (
                  <div class="svc-img">
                    <Image
                      src={card.src}
                      width={card.width}
                      height={card.height}
                      alt={card.alt}
                      widths={imageSpec.widths}
                      sizes={imageSpec.sizes}
                      style={card.imagePos ? `object-position: ${card.imagePos}` : undefined}
                    />
                  </div>
                )}{
                  /* Flush, same rule and the same reason: the tag is absent on
                     five of the six live bands, and a line of its own would add
                     a text node to every tile on all five. */
                  card.tag !== "" && <span class="svc-tag">{card.tag}</span>
                }{
                  (card.body !== "" || hasRowBot) && (
                    <div class="svc-body">
                      {card.body !== "" && <p class="svc-desc" set:html={card.body} />}{
                        hasRowBot && (
                          <div class="svc-row-bot">
                            {card.title !== "" && <h3>{card.title}</h3>}{
                              linked && <ArrowBadge size="xl" variant="ghost-light" />
                            }
                          </div>
                        )
                      }
                    </div>
                  )
                }
              </Fragment>
            );

            return linked ? (
              <a class="svc-card compact" href={card.href} data-vs-arrow>{inner}</a>
            ) : (
              <article class="svc-card compact">{inner}</article>
            );
          })}
        </div>
      ));

    /* cosmetic-dentistry.astro:313-317 and implant-dentistry.astro:368-370 —
       the closing line, inside the same wrap, after the grid. `nested` rides
       along so the guest's copy can take its colour from the host's band; see
       the rule in <style>. */
    const outro = subFootHtml !== "" && (
      <p class:list={["svc-outro", isNested && "nested"]} set:html={subFootHtml} />
    );

    /* THE GUEST. No <section>, no .wrap, no id, no reveal batch — all four
       belong to the host, whose <slot /> this lands in.

       All three conditionals inside the guest's head are flushed `}{`, unlike
       the band's head below: that head reproduces a template where all three
       are always filled, and this one reproduces no template at all, so a guest
       that fills only the heading must not carry two blank lines around it.
       This note is a JS comment rather than a curly-brace comment expression
       for the same family of reason: a comment expression inside the markup
       leaves the newlines around it in the output, and here that output lands
       in the HOST's `.wrap`. */
    if (isNested) {
      return (
        <Fragment>
          {hasNestedHead && (
            <div class="section-head sub">
              {eyebrowText !== "" && (
                <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{eyebrowText}</span>
              )}{headingHtml !== "" && <h3><Fragment set:html={headingHtml} /></h3>}{
                bodyText !== "" && <p>{bodyText}</p>
              }
            </div>
          )}{cards}{outro}
        </Fragment>
      );
    }

    /* THE BAND. `data-vs-reveal-batch` sits on the section exactly as all five
       templates carry it: animations.js animates the section's DIRECT children,
       which is the one `.wrap`, so this is a single fade of the whole band and
       not a per-card stagger. Moving it to the grid would be a different
       animation. */
    return (
      <section class:list={["section", bandClass]} id={id} data-vs-reveal-batch>
        <div class="wrap">
          {hasHead && (
            <div class="section-head">
              {eyebrowText !== "" && (
                <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{eyebrowText}</span>
              )}
              {headingHtml !== "" && <h2><Fragment set:html={headingHtml} /></h2>}
              {bodyText !== "" && <p>{bodyText}</p>}{
                /* services.astro:168-177 — the buttons live INSIDE the head,
                   under the intro. Flush against the body paragraph because
                   three of the six live bands have no CTA at all and must not
                   gain a text node inside their head. */
                hasCtaRow && (
                  <div class="cta-row in-head">
                    <div class="cta-buttons">
                      {hasCta && (
                        <Button variant={primaryVariant} href={cta1.href} hoverLabel={cta1.hover}>
                          {label1}
                        </Button>
                      )}{
                        hasCta2 && (
                          <Button
                            variant={secondaryVariant}
                            href={cta2.href}
                            hoverLabel={cta2.hover}
                          >
                            {label2}
                          </Button>
                        )
                      }
                    </div>{ctaNoteText !== "" && <span class="cta-note">{ctaNoteText}</span>}
                  </div>
                )
              }
            </div>
          )}

          {cards}{outro}
        </div>
      </section>
    );
  })()
}

<style>
  /* ══ WHERE THESE RULES COME FROM ═══════════════════════════════════════════
     Copied out of the four hub sheets — services.css, cosmetic-dentistry.css,
     general-dentistry.css, implant-dentistry.css — with the page prefix
     stripped and each sheet's local aliases resolved back to the `--vs-*`
     tokens they alias ONE FOR ONE (`--sage` -> `--vs-sage`, `--r-lg` ->
     `--vs-r-lg`, and so on: services.css:8-24). Every rendered value is
     unchanged, with the three exceptions named below.

     `--shadow` is NOT one of the one-for-one aliases. All four sheets define it
     locally as a two-layer shadow that is not `--vs-shadow`
     (general-dentistry.css:57), so the feature card's box-shadow is written out
     literally rather than swapped for the token that looks like it.

     SPECIFICITY, which is the thing to get right here rather than the colours.
     Astro 6.1.9 compiles scoped rules to an ATTRIBUTE per compound —
     `.why-card[data-astro-cid-x] h3[data-astro-cid-x]`, read out of
     dist/_astro/PageBlocks.*.css, not assumed. So a two-compound rule of this
     block's comfortably outranks a hub sheet's `.services .thing`, but a
     SINGLE-compound one ties with it at (0,2,0) and bundle order decides. Two
     defects on this project came from that tie. Every rule below that a hub
     sheet contradicts is therefore written with at least two compounds, or as
     `p.svc-outro` / `.svc-grid:not(.nested)`, which lift it clear by one unit.
     The single-compound rules that remain (`.section`, `.wrap`, `.svc-grid`,
     `.svc-grid-4`) carry only declarations all four sheets already agree on, so
     their tie decides nothing.

     THREE PLACES THE HUBS DISAGREE, and what won:

       1. `.svc-outro` max-width — 60ch on cosmetic-dentistry.css:384, 64ch on
          implant-dentistry.css:677. 60ch wins: it is the measure
          `.section-head p` already uses for centred prose on every hub. The
          implant band's closing line re-wraps; not one word moves.
       2. Where a three-across grid collapses to one column — 640px on
          cosmetic-dentistry.css:975 and implant-dentistry.css:1502, 780px on
          services.css:520. 640px wins, two sheets to one; between 640 and 780
          the /services/ grids now read two across instead of one.
       3. `.impd .svc-grid`'s 56px top margin (implant-dentistry.css:573) is a
          consequence of that grid being NESTED under a price table, not of it
          being a grid. It is written here as `.svc-grid.nested`, and
          `:not(.nested)` states the zero explicitly so a standalone grid on
          that page cannot inherit the 56 through a tie.

     Repeated per block rather than shared: a shared sheet is an ancestor by
     another name, and the property that makes a block safe to drop on any page
     is that nothing outside it decides how it looks. */

  /* ---------- band shell ----------
     services.css:116-118 (padding), :126-127 and :153-154 (the two band
     colours). `margin: 0` is FinalBand.astro's rule: the band owns its rhythm,
     so it cannot collapse into whichever band an editor drags above it. */
  .section {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 110px 0;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
  }
  .section.cream {
    background: var(--vs-cream);
  }
  .section.dark {
    background: var(--vs-sage-pale);
  }
  .section.sage {
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section.alt {
    background: var(--vs-charcoal-green);
  }

  /* services.css:112-114 and cosmetic-dentistry.css:195. `--toc-h` falls back
     to 0, NOT to the 68px the detail-page blocks use: this is the first block
     to ship on a hub with no sticky rail (/services/ has none), where a 68px
     fallback would land every anchor jump 68px low. The three hubs that DO have
     a rail declare `--toc-h: 68px` on their own wrapper
     (cosmetic-dentistry.css:36, general-dentistry.css:44,
     implant-dentistry.css:26) and it inherits down, so the fallback is only
     ever read where there is no rail to clear. */
  .section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h, 77px) + var(--toc-h, 0px) + 12px);
  }

  /* services.css:27-31 */
  .wrap {
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  /* ---------- section head ----------
     services.css:33-40, :197-224, :69-78, :578. The h2 is ink, not sage: the
     sheet sets sage in its `:where(h1,h2,h3,h4)` group and flips h2 to ink in
     its end-of-file override (:574-580). Collapsed to the value that renders. */
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  h2,
  .section-head.sub h3 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.2vw, 54px);
  }
  /* `:global()` because the `<em>` arrives through `set:html` and carries no
     scope attribute — a plain `h2 em` compiles to `em[data-astro-cid]`, misses
     it, and drops the accent colour from every heading on the site. */
  h2 :global(em),
  .section-head.sub h3 :global(em) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
    font-weight: 300;
  }

  .section-head {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto 64px;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .section-head .eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    content: "";
    width: 28px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section-head h2 {
    margin: 22px 0 22px;
  }
  .section-head p {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* The guest's head, on PricingTiersBlock's terms: an `<h3>` because the
     host's `<h2>` is already the band's heading, and a smaller size and gap
     because it opens a sub-part of a band rather than a band. Values from
     teeth-whitening.css:601-616, the one sub-head the corpus ships. */
  .section-head.sub {
    margin-bottom: 36px;
  }
  .section-head.sub h3 {
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(22px, 2.4vw, 30px);
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    line-height: 1.18;
    margin: 0 0 14px;
  }
  .section-head.sub p {
    font-size: 16px;
  }

  /* ---------- band inversions ----------
     services.css:130-149 (charcoal) and :157-175 (sage-pale). Scoped to
     `.section-head` and to the outro, never written band-wide: the tiles carry
     their own white-on-photograph palette and the feature cards sit on their
     own white surface, so a band-wide `p { color: #fff }` — which is what the
     hub sheets do — paints white body copy onto a white card the first time an
     editor drags a features grid onto a charcoal band. The sheets get away with
     it because no hub pairs the two. This block has to survive the pairing,
     because making it possible is the entire point of the phase. */
  .section.alt .section-head h2,
  .section.sage .section-head h2 {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.alt .section-head h2 :global(em),
  .section.sage .section-head h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head p,
  .section.sage .section-head p {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow::after,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
  }

  .section.dark .section-head h2 {
    color: var(--vs-ink);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head p,
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    background: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* ---------- the grids ----------
     services.css:229-232 for the base; the column patterns from
     services.css:235-255, cosmetic-dentistry.css:323-326,
     general-dentistry.css:544-548 and implant-dentistry.css:569-580. Only
     `display` and `gap` are written single-compound — all four sheets carry
     those two identically, so the (0,2,0) tie decides nothing. Every
     `grid-template-columns` is on a `.cols-N` compound, which cannot tie. */
  .svc-grid,
  .svc-grid-4 {
    display: grid;
    gap: 22px;
  }

  /* The nested gap: implant-dentistry.css:573. Stated in both directions so a
     standalone grid on that same page cannot pick the 56 up through the tie
     between `.svc-grid[cid]` and `.impd .svc-grid`. `:not()` contributes its
     argument's specificity, so both rules are (0,3,0) and neither ties. */
  .svc-grid.nested,
  .svc-grid-4.nested {
    margin-top: 56px;
  }
  .svc-grid:not(.nested),
  .svc-grid-4:not(.nested) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .svc-grid.cols-2,
  .svc-grid-4.cols-2 {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  }
  .svc-grid.cols-3,
  .svc-grid-4.cols-3 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }
  .svc-grid.cols-4,
  .svc-grid-4.cols-4 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  }
  /* Five across is really six columns with each card spanning two, so that the
     second row reads CENTRED (cols 2-3 and 4-5) rather than left-aligned. The
     pattern and the `nth-child(4)` placement are services.css:243-251, which
     implant-dentistry.css:569-580 carries identically. */
  .svc-grid.cols-5,
  .svc-grid-4.cols-5 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(6, 1fr);
  }
  .svc-grid.cols-5 > .svc-card,
  .svc-grid-4.cols-5 > .svc-feat {
    grid-column: span 2;
  }
  .svc-grid.cols-5 > .svc-card:nth-child(4),
  .svc-grid-4.cols-5 > .svc-feat:nth-child(4) {
    grid-column: 2 / span 2;
  }

  /* ---------- the tile ----------
     services.css:257-382, identical to cosmetic-dentistry.css:327-380 and
     implant-dentistry.css:581-671. Written under `.svc-grid` so every rule is
     at least (0,4,0): the tile's white title has to beat
     `.services .section.dark h3` (0,3,1), which would otherwise drag it to ink
     on the sage-pale band — the hub sheet needs its own :180-185 override for
     exactly this, and a block that relied on the page carrying that override is
     a block that breaks on the next page it lands on.

     `translateZ(0)` + `will-change` (services.css:267-271) keep the card on its
     own GPU layer at rest so the border-radius clip does not briefly fail when
     the hover transform starts. */
  .svc-grid .svc-card {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-lg);
    background: var(--vs-ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    color: #fff;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    isolation: isolate;
    transform: translateZ(0);
    will-change: transform;
    transition: transform 0.4s ease;
  }
  .svc-grid .svc-card:hover {
    transform: translate3d(0, -3px, 0);
  }
  .svc-grid .svc-card .svc-img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
  }
  /* `<Image>` renders a plain `<img>` authored in this file, so it carries the
     scope attribute and needs no `:global`. */
  .svc-grid .svc-card .svc-img img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    transform: scale(1.02);
    transition: transform 1.4s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1);
  }
  .svc-grid .svc-card:hover .svc-img img {
    transform: scale(1.08);
  }
  .svc-grid .svc-card .svc-img::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(20, 28, 16, 0) 0%,
      rgba(20, 28, 16, 0.18) 45%,
      rgba(20, 28, 16, 0.82) 100%
    );
  }
  /* Every tile in the corpus is `.compact` and takes this deeper gradient
     (services.css:303-310); the rule above is the family's base and stays so a
     future non-compact tile is not left with a photograph and no scrim. */
  .svc-grid .svc-card.compact .svc-img::after {
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(20, 28, 16, 0) 0%,
      rgba(20, 28, 16, 0.25) 50%,
      rgba(20, 28, 16, 0.85) 100%
    );
  }
  /* services.css:311-333 */
  .svc-grid .svc-card .svc-tag {
    position: absolute;
    top: 22px;
    left: 22px;
    z-index: 2;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    background: rgba(28, 32, 24, 0.55);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
    backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(160%);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(160%);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 8px 15px;
    font-size: 10.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.24em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #fff;
    font-weight: 500;
    box-shadow:
      0 2px 14px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18),
      inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
  }
  /* services.css:334-347 */
  .svc-grid .svc-card .svc-body {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    margin-top: auto;
    padding: 26px 28px 26px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .svc-grid .svc-card .svc-row-bot {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
  }
  /* services.css:348-357 */
  .svc-grid .svc-card .svc-row-bot h3 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 28px;
    line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: #fff;
    max-width: 14ch;
    margin: 0;
  }
  /* services.css:358-379. The description is clipped to nothing and opens on
     hover or keyboard focus — which is why the tile keeps `:focus-visible`
     even though it is an anchor: the copy has to be reachable without a mouse. */
  .svc-grid .svc-card .svc-desc {
    font-size: 15.5px;
    color: #fff;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-width: 54ch;
    font-weight: 400;
    text-shadow: 0 1px 14px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
    max-height: 0;
    opacity: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 0;
    transform: translateY(6px);
    transition:
      max-height 0.55s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1),
      opacity 0.45s ease 0.05s,
      transform 0.55s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1);
  }
  .svc-grid .svc-card:hover .svc-desc,
  .svc-grid .svc-card:focus-visible .svc-desc {
    max-height: 140px;
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0);
  }
  .svc-grid .svc-card.compact {
    min-height: 380px;
  }

  /* ---------- the feature card ----------
     general-dentistry.css:549-606. Always-visible copy, no hover-reveal: the
     Emergency card's body carries a link, and a link that only appears on hover
     is a link a keyboard and a touch screen cannot find. The box-shadow is that
     sheet's local `--shadow` written out (general-dentistry.css:57) — it is not
     `--vs-shadow`. */
  .svc-grid-4 .svc-feat {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-lg);
    overflow: hidden;
    box-shadow:
      0 24px 60px -30px rgba(40, 40, 30, 0.25),
      0 8px 20px -10px rgba(40, 40, 30, 0.08);
    transition:
      transform 0.35s ease,
      box-shadow 0.35s ease,
      border-color 0.3s ease;
  }
  .svc-grid-4 .svc-feat:hover {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
    box-shadow:
      0 30px 60px -25px rgba(40, 40, 30, 0.28),
      0 12px 24px -12px rgba(40, 40, 30, 0.1);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--vs-sage) 22%, var(--vs-line));
  }
  .svc-grid-4 .svc-feat-img {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 4/3;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--vs-cream-warm), var(--vs-cream));
  }
  .svc-grid-4 .svc-feat-img img {
